welcome back to cbs this morning today  marks exactly  six months six months since a pro-trump  mob  stormed the u.s capitol we all remember  that day more than 500 people have been  arrested and hundreds of investigations  are still underway  so while some of the physical barriers  they've certainly come down around  capitol hill though mental scars remain  for many who work there including the  members of congress  in a story that you'll see here only on  cbs this morning  nicole killian introduces us to a group  of lawmakers  who have developed a shared bond as they  try to cope with what happened on that  awful day everybody  hearing the insurrectionists just the  pounding on the door  the capitol police were locking the  doors with us inside that's where i  realized  there was no way out hearing the shot  come from the speaker's lobby  and i just remember thinking oh my god  six months later the memories are still  vivid for representatives pramila  jayapal  veronica escobar susan wild and jason  crowe  who were among many members trapped in  the house gallery  january 6. how was the gallery group  formed  the very next day we had a caucus call  and i said something about being trapped  in the gallery i mean i think all of us  felt like we had been  forgotten up there and immediately  i got just dozens of texts  from people saying i was in the gallery  too some i knew  you know veronica was right next to me  so congresswoman jayapal started a tax  chain  that has evolved into a support group  with about two dozen lawmakers  what were some of those initial  conversations like for your group  there were a lot of tears there was a  lot of anger what kind of toll  has this taken on each of you personally  i was having some very violent  nightmares that were  deeply disturbing i lost my life partner  to suicide just about two years ago  i had thought i was  past the hardest part of that what i  found out after this  was that i hadn't even begun to deal  with that  it was this viral image of congresswoman  wilde and congressman crow that  triggered flashbacks for him  as an army ranger in iraq and  afghanistan  to be thrust back into that  which i did i kind of switched back into  that mode of being ready to fight  and do whatever was necessary to get us  out i'm a little bit choked up  that picture i believe was taken right  after i got off the phone with my kids  i think i started to have a panic attack  jason reached out took my hand and  started to comfort me then i'll never  forget  on his part have any of you sought  therapy  the tools that my therapist has given me  to deal with  the trauma that has helped me  tremendously  it is important to share with people  that  that trauma takes a toll and that there  is  help out there that there's no shame  in asking for help or having difficult  conversations  for those of us who were people of color  there were different reactions so take  off your member pin  i know i made a conscious decision as  did many of us  to leave the member pin on because it  was the decision  of do you want to be recognized by the  insurrectionist or what happens if you  take it off and you're not recognized by  capitol police and so  there were these beautiful moments in  the group where  there was a recognition of an experience  that we didn't have  but that was the first time in my life  that me  personally was on the receiving end of  the violence  of racism in our country and and since  that  it has allowed me to understand it in a  more visceral sense  instead of just up here also here where  does the  gallery group go from here it's been a  really meaningful group to have  and a wonderful support network i'll  bring a pie to the next gathering  promises promises we have a lot of  inside jokes out of that  jason never brings the pies well you can  see they are a very  tight-knit group and even though they're  all democrats some have tried to  dialogue with republicans  what's interesting is that this goes  beyond therapy now they consult each  other  on just about anything whether that's  legislation or the latest january six  actions like the select committee that  was announced last week jamie  a stronger bond nicole thank you every  time you look at those pictures though  guys it just  it really does take you back to that day  and i keep thinking these were americans  against other americans i can't get over  that  that shot of them of jason crowe holding  susan wilde's hand  i remember that very vividly i'll never  forget i think sometimes we forget when  you look at congress people these are  people  exactly right backgrounds that  compounded trauma and now  exactly you know what do you do with it  what's so terrifying is the complete  collapse of order  and just like that very order regimented  there's a process you're in congress and  then gone at the union capital at the  u.s capitol  i'm glad they have each other i'm glad  they also have pie  well it does make everything better  congressman please bring
 

